// **a `guard` over a result with an owning payload leaks nothing and frees nothing twice**, which is the
// pair of failures this shape is most exposed to: the binding is copied out of storage the result still
// owns, so one missing copy is a double free and one missing drop is a leak.
//
// asserted as a number rather than as a shape - `0` at the end, on every path through both arms, with an
// `array<int32>` on one side and a `string` on the other so neither answer is the other's
function fetch(int32 $n) : result<array<int32>, string>
{
    if ($n < 0) {
        return .error('nothing to fetch');
    }

    array<int32> $rows = [$n, $n + 1, $n + 2];

    return .ok($rows);
}

function total(int32 $n) : int32
{
    $rows = guard fetch($n) else ($why) {
        echo $why;
        return -1;
    }

    int32 $sum = 0;

    foreach ($rows as $row) {
        $sum = $sum + $row;
    }

    return $sum;
}

echo total(10);
echo total(-1);

// and the same through the borrow rather than through a guard, so the copy the guard performs is not the
// only thing keeping the count straight
function first(int32 $n) : int32
{
    result<array<int32>, string> $r = fetch($n);

    if ($r->is_error()) {
        return -2;
    }

    return $r->unwrap()[0];
}

echo first(7);
echo first(-7);
echo mem::live_allocations();
